WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040
Returned from the navy to my job which was held for me at the Department of State.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.440
They wanted me back . . . And to look at LSU which also wanted me back.

3
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.940
So I was on equal leave from both of them.

4
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.140
I chose the low bidder and I came back to the university.

5
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.580
Because at this time,

6
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:33.680
frequency modulation was being developed and I had a great interest in developing it and using it for education and for university extension purposes.

7
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.760
This is what we call FM radio?

8
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.820
FM radio.

9
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.820
And this resulted in my return to the university.

10
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:50.360
It would be about 1947 that I began the construction of station WLSU.

11
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:59.380
I think it was probably the . . . one of the first of the non-commercial frequency modulation stations in the South.

12
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:11.100
And the equipment we got was from the inventor of FM, Major Armstrong, and it was all extremely high-quality stuff.

13
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.600
And we began relaying programs.

14
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:20.140
I remember a symphony that was broadcast in New Jersey by Major Armstrong,

15
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:28.360
picked up by my engineer in New Orleans, sent to our tower at LSU, and rebroadcast.

16
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.920
So we began with a serious interest in music.

17
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.080
One of our most significant programs was “Music in American Life.”

18
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.500
We were an active role in extending Agricultural Extension.

19
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:51.680
We had a special project in which we did a farm and home hour in Cajun, and this was very exciting.

20
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.940
That was before the station—we used commercial radio stations.

21
00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:01.800
It was an exciting time, and I’m sorry the university, in my departure for other things,

22
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.620
somehow lost its radio station just when things began to become very significant.

23
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:16.720
And National Public Radio made that station very important, had it still been in existence.

24
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.160
That is true.

25
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.820
Where was WLSU?

26
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.620
Was it still in the Music and Dramatic Arts building or was it . . . ?

27
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.760
The studio building was over at Dalrymple Drive, right on the edge of the lake.

28
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:35.720
The studio and the transmitter building combination, small but very useful.

29
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.500
I miss it.

30
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.420
I’m sorry it’s gone.

31
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.760
So the building is gone,or the station?

32
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.620
I believe the station is gone.

33
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.720
Probably the building, too.

34
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.800
So how long were you involved in the creation of WLSU?

35
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.220
How long did you stay to help run it?

36
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:59.820
I stayed until 1951, so that would be ’47 to ’51.

37
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.460
And then I was asked to go and help develop public television.
